Small pieces of fabric build up around the reindeer motif in design D. Placing and tucking each piece raises its edges, giving the animal a soft textile surround that catches the light along the folds.

Working with cloth gives you control over the fullness of the finished piece. Even within one colour, the depth of a tuck and the way a loose edge falls can change the surface, giving the wreath a finish that is individual to its maker. The animal motif gives the eye somewhere to settle amongst the textile detail. Keep its outline visible as the surrounding pieces go into place and check the shape from the front before finishing the edges.

Light falling from the side gives each tuck a soft shadow. Hang the finished wreath where daylight can reach it and the texture will show even in parts of the design that use closely matched colours. Unlike a flat printed hanging, the fabric surface has a profile of its own. Allow space in front of it when you choose a position and keep it clear of items that might press the folds flat as the door moves.

Try arranging a few pieces beside one another before tucking them into the base. Two similar shades produce a softer patch of colour, while a change from light to dark makes an edge or small motif stand out.

Once complete, try the decoration against the surface where you intend to display it. A plain background outlines the finished shape, while a nearby side light picks out the surface detail when viewed from an angle. For a shared craft afternoon, one person can sort the next colours while another works on the current section.

To give the finished work as a present, choose a box deep enough to leave the decorated face clear of the lid. A handwritten gift tag can say who made it and when, a small detail that turns the project into a keepsake.
